agency_name': 'U.S. National Science Foundation', 'domain_score': 0.3202769779663728, 'llm_score': 0.52}, {'opportunity_id': '91454c48-930c-4d95-a0dd-8d71233d502b', 'opportunity_title': 'Annual Program Statement (APS) For Fiscal Year 2026', 'agency_name': 'U.S. Mission to Tunisia', 'domain_score': 0.34146609975094633, 'llm_score': 0.25}, {'opportunity_id': '75cd511d-4b7f-4837-9cfd-1c35ae5b2dc5', 'opportunity_title': 'Centers of Research Excellence in Science and Technology - Research Infrastructure for Science and Engineering', 'agency_name': 'U.S. National Science Foundation', 'domain_score': 0.3725043535232544, 'llm_score': 0.22}], 'recommendation': {'email': 'alan.fern@oregonstate.edu', 'k': 10, 'selection': {'source': 'provided_matches', 'ranked_opportunity_ids': ['56e4c5bf-fe1e-4ae1-905b-93a9b53cd8d3', 'd328b689-058f-47a8-b290-fd3f7b36bafd', '599c5796-9954-4aa0-93d2-4a70f0c432ce', '40b954c3-f11e-4dda-986a-ac05dae3bb79', 'd668c4cc-d773-481d-b659-9c0e02fe3801', '7a3287f7-c841-4384-acc9-5194514d701b', 'b39b2236-74f2-466c-875e-8a226a78968c', '7af5a10c-9297-4236-abb2-f7cb8b793498', '91454c48-930c-4d95-a0dd-8d71233d502b', '75cd511d-4b7f-4837-9cfd-1c35ae5b2dc5'], 'updated_match_rows': 0}, 'results': [{'opportunity_id': '56e4c5bf-fe1e-4ae1-905b-93a9b53cd8d3', 'title': 'Foundational Research in Robotics', 'agency': 'U.S. National Science Foundation', 'domain_score': 0.47668472210350965, 'llm_score': 0.98, 'grant_explanation': "The Foundational Research in Robotics (FRR) program, jointly led by the National Science Foundation's CISE and ENG Directorates, supports fundamental research on robotic systems that combine significant computational capability with physical complexity. The program defines a robot as intelligence embodied in an engineered construct capable of processing information, sensing, planning, and moving within or altering its environment. The program emphasizes foundational advances in robotics across the full range of engineering and computer science research challenges. It welcomes interdisciplinary research that explores the interconnected questions of intelligence, computation, and embodiment, or focuses on distinct aspects of these areas when clearly justified within a robotic context. To be responsive to the FRR program, proposals must clearly articulate three key points: (1) the research must focus on a robot or class of robots; (2) the project must endow robots with new and useful capabilities or significantly enhance existing capabilities; and (3) the intellectual contribution must address fundamental gaps in robotics science. Meaningful experimental validation on physical platforms is encouraged. Proposals that do not represent direct fundamental contributions to robotics or are better suited to other NSF programs will not be accepted. Investigators are strongly encouraged to consult with an FRR Program Officer before submission, as non-compliant proposals may be returned without review.", 'grant_brief': "The Foundational Research in Robotics (FRR) program, jointly led by NSF's CISE and ENG Directorates, funds research on robotic systems that combine computational capability with physical complexity. Proposals must demonstrate three key elements: focus on a robot or class of robots, endowment of new or significantly enhanced capabilities, and fundamental intellectual contributions to robotics science. The program emphasizes interdisciplinary research across engineering and computer science, with experimental validation on physical platforms strongly encouraged. Projects must address foundational gaps in robotics rather than applied development or work better suited to other NSF programs.", 'why_match': {'summary': 'Alan Fern is an excellent fit for this **Foundational Research in Robotics** program, with extensive expertise in **reinforcement learning**, **sim-to-real transfer**, and **bipedal locomotion** demonstrated through multiple publications and real-world deployments on the humanoid robot **Digit**.', 'alignment_points': ["Demonstrated **sim-to-real transfer** on physical humanoid robots, including 'Sim-to-Real Learning for Humanoid Box Loco-Manipulation' and 'Learning Vision-Based Bipedal Locomotion for Challenging Terrain' validated on **Digit**.", 'Strong track record in **planning and decision-making** for robotics, with PhD students focused on robotics (Helei Duan, Jonah Seikmann) and thesis work on probabilistic planning and policy learning.', "Proven expertise in **vision-based control** and environmental perception, including 'Learning Multi-Modal Whole-Body Control for Real-World Humanoid Robots' and omnidirectional locomotion using depth images.", 'Research on **explainable AI** and anomaly detection directly supports robot self-assessment and trustworthy autonomous operation, as noted in profile work on xACT paradigm.'], 'risk_gaps': ['Limited evidence of work on **robot hardware failures** or adaptation to mechanical degradation, though anomaly detection research could potentially extend to this domain.', 'Most recent robotics work focuses on **bipedal humanoids**; breadth across other robot classes (manipulators, aerial, multi-agent systems) is not clearly demonstrated.', 'While planning expertise exists (Monte-Carlo planning, probabilistic planning theses), recent publications emphasize **learned controllers** over explicit real-time replanning algorithms for dynamic environments.']}}, {'opportunity_id': 'd328b689-058f-47a8-b290-fd3f7b36bafd', 'title': 'Tactical Behaviors for Autonomous Maneuver', 'agency': 'Dept of the Army -- Materiel Command', 'domain_score': 0.48977640332124306, 'llm_score': 0.95, 'grant_explanation': 'The Tactical Behaviors for Autonomous Maneuver Collaborative Research Program (TBAM-CRP) Cycle 2 is a U.S. Army Research Laboratory funding opportunity focused on developing coordinated and individual behaviors for small groups of autonomous agents to operate in military-relevant environments. The grant emphasizes research into tactical maneuvers for teams of robotics and autonomous systems (RAS) that can learn both doctrinal and novel tactics for maneuvering in complex, non-permissive terrain against capable adversaries. **What the Grant Is About:** The TBAM-CRP seeks to advance fundamental understanding of how teams of autonomous systems can operate alongside future Army forces in multi-domain operations. The Cycle 2 sprint topic is "Coordinated maneuver against adversaries," specifically focusing on scenarios where autonomous teams must make tactical decisions as they approach contact with an enemy position. The research aims to develop solutions that exploit terrain and cover, operate in complex environments with adversaries, synchronize motion across teams, and adapt behaviors dynamically based on operational phases and changing conditions. **What It Emphasizes:** - Development of resilient, coordinated autonomous team behaviors that can operate under extreme uncertainty and disruption - Integration with the ARL Autonomy Stack software suite and simulation environments - Collaboration among academia, industry, and government partners - Demonstration of capabilities in sophisticated simulations of realistic military environments - Transition of research outcomes to higher technology readiness levels through ARL test facilities - Emphasis on decentralized algorithms that are robust to degraded communications and asset loss - Intellectual property approaches that foster collaboration and advance open-source or government-owned autonomy solutions **What Capabilities It Expects:** Recipients are expected to develop autonomous systems capable of: - Maneuvering through complex terrains (urban, jungle, forest, desert, off-road) - Detecting and responding to adversarial contact with tactical advantage - Gathering and managing information about adversary disposition and capabilities - Operating in teams of approximately 10 units with coordinated decision-making - Adapting tactics based on terrain, risk levels, and mission constraints - Demonstrating results in simulation with potential higher-TRL experimentation on surrogate platforms - Participating in mandatory biweekly collaboration meetings and annual program reviews - Contributing to a collaborative ecosystem of research performers **Funding and Structure:** The grant provides cooperative agreements for a 24-month pilot base period with up to two optional 12-month extensions. Cycle 2 has $2.1M total annual funding with multiple awards expected. Cost sharing is not required. Recipients must address technical approach, collaboration plans, intellectual property strategy, and detailed budgets including direct labor, fringe benefits, equipment, travel, and other direct costs.', 'grant_brief': 'This grant funds research on coordinated autonomous maneuver tactics for small robot teams operating in complex military environments against adversaries. Cycle 2 focuses on "Preparation for Contact"—developing algorithms and behaviors for teams to achieve tactical advantage when encountering enemy positions in terrain-rich, non-permissive environments. Recipients will develop and demonstrate solutions in simulation using the ARL Autonomy Stack and Unity-based testbeds, with potential transition to physical platforms at ARL facilities. The program emphasizes collaborative academia-industry-government partnerships with mandatory biweekly meetings with ARL researchers and annual capstone demonstrations.', 'why_match': {'summary': 'Alan Fern is a strong fit for this Army tactical autonomy program due to his extensive background in **automated planning**, **reinforcement learning**, and **multi-agent coordination** for complex sequential decision-making problems, including prior DARPA-funded work on tactical reasoning in real-time strategy games.', 'alignment_points': ['Proven expertise in **automated planning** and **adversarial reasoning** through DARPA-funded research on tactical problems in real-time strategy games requiring visual interpretation, quick decision making, and reasoning about adversaries.', 'Strong foundation in **reinforcement learning** for robotics demonstrated by work on bipedal locomotion controllers (Cassie robot) and sim-to-real transfer, directly applicable to autonomous maneuver systems.', 'Extensive student supervision in **probabilistic planning** and **sequential decision making** (15+ PhD/MS theses), including topics like Monte-Carlo planning, policy learning, and behavior constraints for autonomous systems.', 'Research on **explainable AI** and trustworthy autonomous systems through the xACT paradigm, addressing critical verification needs for safety-critical military applications.'], 'risk_gaps': ['No documented experience with **multi-agent tactical coordination** in military-relevant scenarios or **terrain-aware mobility** for ground vehicles in complex outdoor environments.', 'Publications focus on **agricultural modeling** and **sports video analysis** rather than defense robotics, GPS-denied navigation, or distributed autonomous systems for maneuver warfare.', 'Missing evidence of work with **ROS/ROS2 architectures**, **SLAM systems**, or integration with government autonomy stacks required for ARL collaboration and experimentation events.']}}, {'opportunity_id': '599c5796-9954-4aa0-93d2-4a70f0c432ce', 'title': 'Energy, Power, Control, and Networks', 'agency': 'U.S. National Science Foundation', 'domain_score': 0.4453574484160604, 'llm_score': 0.88, 'grant_explanation': 'The Energy, Power, Control, and Networks (EPCN) Program, funded by the U.S. National Science Foundation, supports innovative research in modeling, optimization, learning, adaptation, and control of networked multi-agent systems. The program emphasizes higher-level decision making, dynamic resource allocation, and risk management under uncertainty, sub-system failures, and stochastic disturbances. Key Focus Areas: The program invests in novel machine learning algorithms, adaptive dynamic programming, brain-like networked architectures with real-time learning capabilities, and neuromorphic engineering. EPCN targets emerging technologies and applications in energy, transportation, robotics, and biomedical devices and systems. Specific Emphasis: EPCN places particular emphasis on electric power systems, including generation, transmission, storage, and renewable energy integration into the grid. The program also covers power electronics and drives, battery management systems, hybrid and electric vehicles, and the interplay between power systems and regulatory, economic, and consumer behavior factors. Additional focus areas include microgrids, energy-efficient buildings and communities, power grid cybersecurity, and market design. Expected Capabilities: Researchers are expected to develop capabilities in control systems, distributed control and optimization, networked multi-agent systems, cyber-physical control systems, and data-enabled learning and decision-making. The program expects advances in machine learning algorithms, intelligent systems, energy harvesting and storage, advanced power electronics, and resilient grid operation and monitoring.', 'grant_brief': 'The Energy, Power, Control, and Networks (EPCN) Program funds research on modeling, optimization, learning, and control of networked multi-agent systems with applications to energy, transportation, robotics, and biomedical systems. The program emphasizes electric power systems including renewable energy integration, grid resilience, power electronics, and electric vehicles, alongside machine learning algorithms and neuromorphic engineering. Research areas span dynamic data-enabled learning, distributed control, cyber-physical systems, and understanding the interplay between power systems and regulatory/economic structures. EPCN supports innovative work addressing uncertainty, system failures, and real-time adaptive control in complex networked environments.', 'why_match': {'summary': "Alan Fern's expertise in **reinforcement learning** and **sequential decision-making** offers methodological overlap with the grant's focus on **machine learning algorithms** and **adaptive control**, but his research is entirely focused on agricultural applications rather than energy, power systems, or robotics.", 'alignment_points': ['**Reinforcement learning** for sequential decision-making demonstrated in WOFOSTGym (2025) for optimizing management under **partial observability** and delayed feedback.', "**Hybrid modeling** combining neural networks with biophysical models in crop prediction (2026) aligns with grant's interest in **data-enabled learning** and control.", "**Multi-agent systems** experience shown in WOFOSTGym's multi-farm settings relates to grant's focus on **networked multi-agent systems** and distributed control."], 'risk_gaps': ['No evidence of research in **power systems**, **microgrids**, **renewable energy integration**, or any electric grid applications central to the grant.', 'Lacks expertise in **power electronics**, **battery management**, **electric vehicles**, or energy storage devices emphasized by the program.', 'No demonstrated work in **market design**, **consumer behavior**, **regulatory policy**, or **cybersecurity** for power grids.']}}, {'opportunity_id': '40b954c3-f11e-4dda-986a-ac05dae3bb79', 'title': 'Automated Discovery for Design and Control of Turbulent Systems (AutoDIDACTS)', 'agency': 'DARPA - Defense Sciences Office', 'domain_score': 0.3165054430660347, 'llm_score': 0.72, 'grant_explanation': 'The AutoDIDACTS (Automated Discovery for Design and Control of Turbulent Systems) grant is a DARPA Defense Sciences Office initiative focused on developing data-informed approaches to discover generalizable models and principles for aeronautical design and optimal control of turbulent systems. **What the Grant Is About:** AutoDIDACTS seeks to revolutionize aeronautical design by combining precision experimental data from compact turbulence simulators with AI-based algorithms to automatically discover macroscale design principles and surrogate models. Rather than relying on computationally expensive simulations or limited wind-tunnel data, the program emphasizes extracting universal emergent laws from high-quality experimental data to enable rapid design exploration and optimization across wide parameter regimes. **What It Emphasizes:** 1. **Data-Informed Discovery**: Using precision ground-truth experimental data as the foundation for developing predictive models, rather than computational data alone 2. **Generalizability**: Models and principles must demonstrate accuracy and applicability across wide ranges of flow conditions, Reynolds numbers, Mach numbers, and other parameters 3. **Interpretability**: Design principles must be traceable to established scientific and aeronautical principles, not just "black box" predictions 4. **Revolutionary Advances**: The program explicitly excludes evolutionary improvements and seeks transformative breakthroughs in aeronautical design capabilities 5. **Multi-Physics Integration**: Approaches should support incremental inclusion of multi-physics models for multi-objective optimization and inverse design paradigms **What Capabilities It Expects:** - Development and calibration of compact, tunable turbulence simulators capable of generating large volumes of precision experimental data - Algorithms for automated discovery of macroscale design principles and surrogate models from experimental data - Demonstration of model accuracy and generalizability through independent verification and validation - Ability to predict aerodynamic performance across unsteady flow regimes, transonic conditions, supersonic/hypersonic flows, and other operationally relevant scenarios - Capability for inverse design (working backward from desired aerodynamic properties to optimal designs) - Integration with existing aeronautical design tools and methodologies - Collaborative capability with other program performers and government teams for data sharing and validation The 48-month program is structured as a 24-month Phase I (focused on data acquisition and model development) followed by a 24-month Phase II (demonstrating solutions to specific challenge problems in areas such as micro-air vehicles, transonic flow, supersonic/hypersonic regimes, or atmospheric turbulence effects on optical communications).', 'grant_brief': 'AutoDIDACTS is a DARPA 48-month research program (24-month Phase I base + 24-month Phase II option) seeking to develop data-informed macroscale design principles and surrogate models for aeronautical design and turbulent flow control. Teams must combine expertise in compact turbulence simulators for precision ground-truth experimental data, AI-based algorithms for automated discovery of design principles, and demonstration of end-of-program challenge problems across regimes including low-Reynolds-number, transonic, supersonic/hypersonic, and optical communications applications. Phase I focuses on developing and validating these data-informed models with rigorous uncertainty quantification and interpretability, while Phase II will demonstrate solutions to specific challenge problems in DoD-relevant aeronautical and turbulence control domains.', 'why_match': {'summary': "Alan Fern's expertise in **reinforcement learning** and **reduced-order modeling** for robotic control offers transferable methodologies, but his work focuses on bipedal robotics rather than **turbulent flow systems** or aeronautical design, creating a significant domain mismatch.", 'alignment_points': ['Demonstrated **reduced-order modeling** expertise via single rigid-body models (SRBM) for bipedal robots, analogous to surrogate modeling approaches needed for turbulent systems.', "Proven **sim-to-real transfer** and **reinforcement learning** for dynamic control in 'Optimizing Bipedal Locomotion' and 'Sim-to-Real Learning for Humanoid Box Loco-Manipulation' publications.", 'Strong background in **optimization** and **control** for complex nonlinear systems, evidenced by Guinness World Record for fastest 100m bipedal robot.'], 'risk_gaps': ['No evidence of experience with **turbulent flow physics**, aerodynamics, or fluid dynamics modeling, which are central to AutoDIDACTS.', 'Lacks demonstrated expertise in **physics-informed neural networks** or **deep operator networks** specifically for partial differential equations like Navier-Stokes.', 'No publications or projects involving **aeronautical design**, wind tunnel experiments, or turbulence simulators required by the grant.']}}, {'opportunity_id': 'd668c4cc-d773-481d-b659-9c0e02fe3801', 'title': 'NSF Trailblazer Engineering Impact Award', 'agency': 'U.S. National Science Foundation', 'domain_score': 0.41006872593946975, 'llm_score': 0.68, 'grant_explanation': 'The NSF Trailblazer Engineering Impact Award (TRAILBLAZER) is a program that supports individual investigators pursuing novel, transformative research projects in emerging engineering frontiers. The program emphasizes supporting engineers and scientists who leverage their distinctive track record of innovation and creativity to pivot into distinctly new research areas—distinct from their previous or current work. What the grant emphasizes: - Novel research with potential to innovatively address new areas of fundamental or applied research - Projects that catalyze development of new industries or capabilities, increasing U.S. leadership - Significant progress toward addressing national needs or grand challenges, particularly in priority areas including artificial intelligence (AI), bioengineering, quantum engineering, robotics, and nuclear engineering - Bold, innovative, and potentially risky approaches using unconventional hypotheses - Convergence of engineering and science domains - Workforce development and participation of emerging researchers What capabilities it expects: - Principal investigators must demonstrate a strong track record of research excellence, creativity, and impactful innovation - PIs must show capacity to form and lead effective multidisciplinary teams - Ability to pivot to new research directions with transformative potential - Leadership vision for conducting potentially transformative research - Capacity to design and lead projects addressing significant research problems - Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively across diverse disciplines The program provides up to $3,000,000 over three years per award, with a minimum of 5 awards anticipated from a $15,000,000 FY 2026 budget. All funded investigators form an NSF TRAILBLAZER cohort and are expected to participate in annual meetings and present their research results.', 'grant_brief': 'The NSF Trailblazer Engineering Impact Award supports individual investigators pursuing novel, transformative research projects in emerging engineering frontiers such as AI, bioengineering, quantum engineering, robotics, and nuclear engineering. PIs must demonstrate a distinctive track record of innovation and creativity while proposing research that represents a significant departure from their previous work into bold new areas with potential to address national needs or grand challenges. Awards of up to $3 million over three years are anticipated for a minimum of 5 projects, with funded investigators expected to participate in an annual TRAILBLAZER cohort meeting. The program emphasizes unconventional hypotheses, convergence of engineering and science domains, and workforce development rather than detailed preliminary data or experimental plans.', 'why_match': {'summary': 'Alan Fern demonstrates strong expertise in **AI and robotics** with applications to agriculture and bipedal locomotion, but his work appears to be extensions of ongoing research rather than pivots to distinctly new areas, which is a core TRAILBLAZER expectation.', 'alignment_points': ['Recent work on **hybrid modeling** for crop prediction (2026) and **soil water content forecasting** (2024) demonstrates convergence of **AI with agricultural science** for national food security challenges.', "Extensive **robotics** research including bipedal locomotion and sim-to-real reinforcement learning (2022-2025) aligns with TRAILBLAZER's priority areas in **AI and robotics**.", 'Cross-disciplinary work applying **developmental psychology methods** to evaluate learned robot behaviors (2025) shows capacity for convergent research across engineering and science domains.'], 'risk_gaps': ['Recent publications on **crop prediction, soil forecasting, and bipedal robotics** appear to extend ongoing research programs rather than represent a **pivot to distinctly new areas**.', 'No clear evidence of a track record in areas outside **AI/machine learning/robotics**, limiting ability to demonstrate departure from current research as TRAILBLAZER explicitly requires.', 'Agricultural AI applications and robotics work seem to be active research areas, potentially overlapping with **prior or concurrent funding** which would preclude TRAILBLAZER eligibility.']}}, {'opportunity_id': '7a3287f7-c841-4384-acc9-5194514d701b', 'title': 'Disability and Rehabilitation Engineering', 'agency': 'U.S. National Science Foundation', 'domain_score': 0.40193867683410933, 'llm_score': 0.62, 'grant_explanation': 'The Disability and Rehabilitation Engineering (DARE) program, administered by the U.S. National Science Foundation, supports fundamental engineering research aimed at improving the quality of life for persons with disabilities. The program funds the development of new theories, methodologies, technologies, and devices to address developmental, cognitive, hearing, mobility, visual, self-care, and independent living disabilities. What the grant emphasizes: - Fundamental engineering knowledge that advances understanding of specific disabilities, pathological motion, or injury mechanisms - Transformative outcomes and high-risk/high-reward proposals that surpass incremental technological improvements - Research directed toward characterization, restoration, rehabilitation, and substitution of human functional ability or cognition - Interaction between persons with disabilities and their environment - Participatory design and inclusion of trainees with disabilities in research and broader impacts Areas of particular interest include neuroengineering, rehabilitation robotics, brain-inspired assistive or rehabilitative systems, theoretical or computational methods, and novel models of functional recovery including artificial physiological systems. What capabilities it expects: - Proposals must demonstrate novelty and potentially transformative nature compared to previous work - Clear articulation of importance in terms of engineering science and potential societal or industry impact - Feasibility studies involving human volunteers may be supported, but clinical trials are not funded - Typical award duration is up to three years for unsolicited proposals; CAREER proposals are five years - Single-investigator budgets typically support one graduate student and up to one month of PI time annually - Proposals must comply with NSF guidelines or will be returned without review - The program does not support commercialization of products; product development should be directed to NSF SBIR/STTR programs - PIs are encouraged to contact the Program Director before submission for innovative proposals outside specified interest areas', 'grant_brief': 'The NSF Disability and Rehabilitation Engineering (DARE) program funds fundamental engineering research that improves quality of life for persons with disabilities through development of new theories, technologies, and devices. Projects must advance knowledge about specific disabilities, pathological motion, or injury mechanisms, with emphasis on neuroengineering, rehabilitation robotics, brain-inspired assistive systems, and novel functional recovery models. The program prioritizes high-risk/high-reward proposals that achieve transformative outcomes rather than incremental improvements, and encourages participatory design with trainees with disabilities. Typical awards support single investigators for up to three years with funding for one graduate student and PI time, though CAREER proposals (five years) and other grant types are also encouraged.', 'why_match': {'summary': "Alan Fern's expertise in **bipedal locomotion** and **rehabilitation robotics** offers partial relevance to the DARE program, but his work focuses on robotic platforms rather than **human functional recovery** or disability-specific interventions.", 'alignment_points': ["Strong **rehabilitation robotics** foundation demonstrated in 'Dynamic Bipedal Maneuvers through Sim-to-Real Reinforcement Learning' and 'Interruptive Language Control of Bipedal Locomotion' on Cassie robot.", "Advanced **vision-based locomotion** methods in 'Learning Vision-Based Bipedal Locomotion for Challenging Terrain' show novel computational approaches for adaptive movement systems.", "**Sim-to-real reinforcement learning** expertise in 'Sim-to-Real Learning for Humanoid Box Loco-Manipulation' demonstrates transferable methodologies for assistive device control."], 'risk_gaps': ['No evidence of research addressing **human disability populations**, pathological motion, or injury mechanisms as required by DARE program focus.', 'Work centers on **robotic platform development** (Cassie, Digit) rather than artificial physiological systems modeling **functional recovery** in humans.', 'Missing demonstrated expertise in **disability domains** (cognitive, visual, self-care) or integration with rehabilitation science and clinical validation.']}}, {'opportunity_id': 'b39b2236-74f2-466c-875e-8a226a78968c', 'title': 'Mind, Machine and Motor Nexus', 'agency': 'U.S. National Science Foundation', 'domain_score': 0.34077893605483345, 'llm_score': 0.58, 'grant_explanation': 'The Mind, Machine, and Motor Nexus (M3X) program, funded by the U.S. National Science Foundation, supports fundamental research on bidirectional interactions between intelligent engineered systems and humans in physics-based environments. The program emphasizes enhancing safety, productivity, and well-being through human-machine collaboration in complex and dynamic settings such as elder care, disaster response, and dynamic workplaces. The program focuses on intelligent engineered systems—physical, virtual, or hybrid systems that collect data, make informed decisions, and take actions to enhance safety and efficiency. These systems must operate within physics-based environments exhibiting recognizable physical behaviors such as gravity, friction, force, and inertia. M3X expects proposals to advance knowledge of bidirectional human-machine interactions and make significant contributions to at least one of three areas: 1. **Conceptual Frameworks and Theoretical Modeling**: Development of new conceptual, mathematical, or computational frameworks for understanding human-engineered system interactions, including models of cognition, perception, and behavior, as well as safe operating conditions and performance capability limits. 2. **Dynamic Interaction Analysis and Simulation**: Investigation of bidirectional interaction phenomena in physical, virtual, or hybrid environments, including learning, co-adaptation, cooperation, and competition, with novel experimental paradigms to evaluate processes and performance. 3. **Innovative Technologies for Enhanced Interaction**: Development of methods, tools, and technologies enabling novel or improved bidirectional interaction, including task environments, interaction modalities and interfaces, advanced measurement and instrumentation methods, and real-time integration of multi-modal sensorimotor data. The program strongly encourages proposals establishing new perspectives and paradigms across multiple areas and welcomes pre-submission feedback from Program Directors.', 'grant_brief': 'The Mind, Machine, and Motor Nexus (M3X) program funds fundamental research on bidirectional interactions between humans and intelligent engineered systems operating in physics-based environments. Research must advance knowledge in at least one of three areas: conceptual frameworks for understanding human-machine interaction, dynamic interaction analysis and simulation in physical/virtual/hybrid settings, or innovative technologies that enable novel interaction modalities. The program targets complex, dynamic applications such as elder care, disaster response, and dynamic workplaces, emphasizing safety, productivity, and well-being. Proposals should explore physical and cognitive principles underlying human-machine collaboration and develop foundational theories, interaction models, and technological innovations that enhance adaptability and intuitiveness.', 'why_match': {'summary': "Alan Fern's expertise in **reinforcement learning** and **autonomous robotic systems** shows limited connection to the M3X program's core focus on **bidirectional human-machine interaction**. His work primarily addresses autonomous robot control in physics-based environments rather than collaborative or interactive systems involving humans.", 'alignment_points': ["Strong foundation in **vision-based locomotion** demonstrated in 'Learning Vision-Based Bipedal Locomotion for Challenging Terrain' with **sim-to-real transfer** and multimodal sensory integration.", 'Proven capability in **physics-based environments** through bipedal robot work on Cassie, including Guinness World Record for 100m dash, showing mastery of gravity, friction, and force dynamics.', "Experience with **adaptive systems** in 'Hybrid Modeling Framework for Crop Prediction' using neural networks to dynamically calibrate biophysical models based on environmental data."], 'risk_gaps': ['No evidence of research involving **bidirectional human-machine interaction**, which is the central focus of M3X; all work centers on autonomous systems.', 'Absence of publications addressing **human cognition, perception, or behavior** in collaborative settings such as elder care, disaster response, or dynamic workplaces.', 'No demonstrated work on **human-robot collaboration**, co-adaptation, or interfaces enabling humans to interact with intelligent systems in real-time.']}}, {'opportunity_id': '7af5a10c-9297-4236-abb2-f7cb8b793498', 'title': 'Engineering Design and Systems Engineering', 'agency': 'U.S. National Science Foundation', 'domain_score': 0.3202769779663728, 'llm_score': 0.52, 'grant_explanation': 'The Engineering Design and Systems Engineering (EDSE) program, funded by the U.S. National Science Foundation, supports fundamental research that advances design science and systems science through the creation of new knowledge about the design of engineered artifacts. These artifacts encompass devices, products, processes, platforms, materials, organizations, systems, and systems of systems. The program emphasizes a holistic approach to design, treating it as a system where designers, the artifacts they create, the methods they employ, and the surrounding environment are all subject to rigorous scientific inquiry. It strongly encourages multidisciplinary proposals that combine expertise in design science and systems science with knowledge from other domains, including social, behavioral, computational, and natural sciences. Competitive proposals are expected to be firmly grounded in theory, demonstrate clear potential to improve design practices, and include rigorous plans to assess the performance and effectiveness of proposed research methods across all involved domains. The program supports fundamental contributions in areas including design representation, design optimization, design validation, mechanism design, robotics and intelligent system design, design of engineered materials systems, design cognition, design collaboration, data science and artificial intelligence in design, design in under-resourced communities, immersive design, and design at extreme scales and extreme environments. Prospective investigators are encouraged to contact the Program Director before proposal submission.', 'grant_brief': 'The Engineering Design and Systems Engineering (EDSE) program from the NSF funds fundamental research that advances design science and systems science through rigorous investigation of how engineered artifacts—including devices, products, processes, materials, and systems—are designed. The program emphasizes multidisciplinary collaboration between design science experts and specialists in social, behavioral, computational, and natural sciences to develop new knowledge about design representation, optimization, validation, and cognition. Competitive proposals must be grounded in theory, demonstrate potential to improve design practices, and include rigorous assessment plans. The program particularly supports research in areas such as robotics, intelligent systems, AI in design, design in under-resourced communities, and design at extreme scales or environments.', 'why_match': {'summary': "Alan Fern's expertise in **reinforcement learning** for **robotics and intelligent system design** aligns with EDSE's focus on AI-driven design, but his work lacks direct engagement with **design science theory** or multidisciplinary design collaboration beyond computational domains.", 'alignment_points': ['Strong **robotics and intelligent system design** expertise demonstrated through real-world humanoid control (Masked Humanoid Controller on Digit V3, 2025) and bipedal locomotion (Cassie sim-to-real transfer, 2022).', 'Proven **data science and AI in design** capabilities via hybrid modeling for crop prediction (60% phenology accuracy improvement, 2026) and time-series imputation using **diffusion models** (2024-2025).', 'Interdisciplinary approach integrating **developmental psychology** with robotics evaluation (infant-inspired locomotion study, 2025) and adaptive control under faults (wave energy converter Bayesian RL, 2023).'], 'risk_gaps': ['No evidence of **design science theory** grounding or systematic inquiry into design representation, design cognition, or design collaboration as emphasized by EDSE.', 'Limited demonstration of **extreme scale design** (nano to infrastructure) or **systems-of-systems** engineering; work focuses on individual robotic artifacts and agricultural models.', 'Lacks explicit **multidisciplinary collaboration** with social or behavioral scientists in design contexts; interdisciplinary work remains primarily computational and biological.']}}, {'opportunity_id': '91454c48-930c-4d95-a0dd-8d71233d502b', 'title': 'Annual Program Statement (APS) For Fiscal Year 2026', 'agency': 'U.S. Mission to Tunisia', 'domain_score': 0.34146609975094633, 'llm_score': 0.25, 'grant_explanation': "This grant, offered by the U.S. Mission to Tunisia through the Public Diplomacy Section, supports results-oriented programs that strengthen U.S.-Tunisian relations while commemorating the 250th anniversary of U.S. independence (2026) and 230 years of U.S.-Tunisian friendship (2027). **What the Grant Emphasizes:** - All programs must include an American element or connection with U.S. experts, organizations, or institutions - Programs should promote understanding of U.S. policies, perspectives, society, and culture - Proposals covering multiple governorates or fostering alumni collaboration are encouraged - No funding for construction-related activities **Expected Capabilities and Program Areas:** 1. **Economic Prosperity and Entrepreneurship**: Programs connecting U.S. companies with Tunisian partners, facilitating business-to-business networking, investment matchmaking, and helping Tunisian businesses meet U.S. market standards 2. **Celebrating America's 250th Anniversary and U.S.-Tunisian Partnership**: Cultural and artistic initiatives, performances, exhibitions, sports diplomacy, and community service activities celebrating U.S. achievements and bilateral friendship 3. **Innovation and Collaboration in Science and Technology**: Programs promoting AI adoption, solar eclipse collaboration (2027), university-industry partnerships, and STEM research in cybersecurity, space, energy, water security, food security, smart agriculture, health technology, and AI 4. **Mega Decade of Sports**: Initiatives leveraging the 2026 FIFA World Cup, 2028 Summer Olympics, and 2034 Winter Olympics to strengthen people-to-people ties and demonstrate U.S. excellence in sports and tourism 5. **Excellence in Education and English Teaching**: Programs showcasing American educational leadership, specialized English courses for business opportunities, and innovative collaborations between U.S. and Tunisian higher education institutions **Eligible Activities Include**: Expert lectures and seminars, sports and culinary diplomacy, art exhibitions, cultural heritage conservation using VR/AR technology, hackathons and STEM camps, university partnerships, English language programs, podcasts, and professional exchanges.", 'grant_brief': "The U.S. Mission to Tunisia is seeking proposals for public diplomacy programs that strengthen U.S.-Tunisian relations while commemorating America's 250th independence anniversary (2026) and 230 years of bilateral friendship (2027). Eligible initiatives must feature American expertise or partnerships and focus on one of five priority areas: economic prosperity and entrepreneurship, celebrating America's achievements, innovation and science/technology collaboration, sports diplomacy tied to major U.S.-hosted sporting events, or excellence in education and English teaching. Programs should foster business opportunities, cultural exchange, STEM innovation, and people-to-people connections, with preference given to initiatives outside Greater Tunis or spanning multiple regions. Funding cannot support construction activities.", 'why_match': {'summary': "Alan Fern's expertise in **smart agriculture** and **AI-driven crop prediction** offers limited fit for a U.S.-Tunisia public diplomacy grant focused on cultural exchange, business matchmaking, and celebrating bilateral ties, as his work lacks the required **international partnership** and **cultural diplomacy** dimensions.", 'alignment_points': ["**Smart agriculture** research in 'Hybrid Modeling Framework for Crop Prediction' and 'Learning Extended Forecasts of Soil Water Content' could support food security STEM collaboration priorities.", '**Deep learning** and **multi-task learning** methods in crop phenology and cold hardiness prediction demonstrate U.S. leadership in AI and agricultural technology innovation.', "**Transfer learning** framework in 'Transfer Learning via Auxiliary Labels' showcases advanced AI methodologies applicable to data-limited agricultural settings relevant to Tunisia."], 'risk_gaps': ['No evidence of **Tunisia-specific partnerships**, international collaboration experience, or ties to Tunisian universities, research parks, or agricultural enterprises.', 'Research focuses on **U.S. viticulture** (grape/wine production) with no demonstrated experience in **business-to-business networking**, investment matchmaking, or commercial partnership facilitation.', 'No background in **public diplomacy**, cultural exchange programming, or activities celebrating U.S.-Tunisia friendship and the America250 anniversary themes central to this grant.']}}, {'opportunity_id': '75cd511d-4b7f-4837-9cfd-1c35ae5b2dc5', 'title': 'Centers of Research Excellence in Science and Technology - Research Infrastructure for Science and Engineering', 'agency': 'U.S. National Science Foundation', 'domain_score': 0.3725043535232544, 'llm_score': 0.22, 'grant_explanation': 'The CREST-RISE (Centers of Research Excellence in Science and Technology - Research Infrastructure for Science and Engineering) grant from the National Science Foundation supports minority-serving institutions (MSIs) in expanding their research capacity and doctoral education in STEM fields. **What the grant is about:** CREST-RISE enhances research capabilities at MSIs through the integration of education and research, with a focus on increasing the production of STEM research doctoral students, particularly those from groups underrepresented in STEM. The program operates through three tracks: DPSI (STEM Doctoral Programs Support Initiative), RAD (Research Advancement and Development), and E&I (Equipment & Instrumentation). **What it emphasizes:** - Increasing the number of STEM research doctoral programs at MSIs - Producing more STEM research doctoral graduates, especially from underrepresented groups - Expanding institutional research capacity to improve doctoral student graduation rates - Integration of research and education with evidence-based recruitment and retention strategies - Institutional transformation and long-term sustainability of research capabilities - Focus on areas of national interest including artificial intelligence, data science, advanced materials, cybersecurity, quantum information sciences, and clean energy **What capabilities it expects:** - Institutions must be MSIs with 50% or more U.S. resident students from underrepresented minority groups and be Emerging Research Institutions (less than $50 million in annual research expenditures) - Faculty leadership must hold full-time positions in NSF-supported STEM disciplines - Comprehensive strategies for recruiting, mentoring, and retaining diverse doctoral students - Strong institutional commitment with provost-level support and financial sustainability plans - Integration with existing NSF-funded awards and partnerships with other MSIs - Formal evaluation plans with independent evaluators to assess progress and impact - Professional development, ethics training, and career development opportunities for students and faculty - Alignment with institutional strategic plans and departmental long-term goals', 'grant_brief': 'The CREST-RISE program supports minority-serving institutions (MSIs) and emerging research institutions in building STEM research capacity and increasing the production of research doctoral students, particularly from underrepresented groups. The program offers three tracks: DPSI (up to $2M over 5 years) to create or strengthen doctoral programs, RAD (up to $1M over 5 years) for junior faculty research development, and E&I (up to $500K for 1 year) for equipment acquisition. Eligible institutions must have 50% or more U.S. resident students from underrepresented minority groups and less than $50M in annual research expenditures. Projects must integrate education and research, demonstrate institutional commitment to sustainability, and employ evidence-based strategies for recruiting and retaining diverse doctoral students in NSF-supported STEM fields.', 'why_match': {'summary': "Alan Fern demonstrates strong expertise in **artificial intelligence** and **robotics**, which are priority areas for CREST-RISE, but the grant specifically targets **minority-serving institutions** (MSIs) with doctoral programs, and there is no evidence that Oregon State University qualifies as an MSI or that Fern's work involves institutional capacity-building for underrepresented groups.", 'alignment_points': ['Research in **AI and robotics** directly matches CREST-RISE priority areas, evidenced by publications on **reinforcement learning** for humanoid control (Masked Humanoid Controller, 2024-2025).', 'Demonstrated **sim-to-real transfer** capabilities with hardware platforms (Digit V3, Cassie) align with cutting-edge research infrastructure development goals.', "Work on **learned bipedal locomotion** and whole-body control represents transformative approaches in robotics, as shown in 'Dynamic Bipedal Maneuvers through Sim-to-Real Reinforcement Learning' (2022)."], 'risk_gaps': ['No evidence that Oregon State University is a **minority-serving institution** (MSI) with 50%+ underrepresented minority enrollment, a core eligibility criterion.', 'Faculty profile lacks documentation of **doctoral student mentoring** focused on underrepresented groups in STEM or institutional capacity-building activities.', 'No indication of involvement in **institutional strategic planning** for doctoral program development or sustainability initiatives required by CREST-RISE.']}}]}, 'grant_explanation': None, 'recommendation_error': None}2026-05-15 13:23:51,822 | INFO | werkzeug | 127.0.0.1 - - [15/May/2026 13:23:51] "[31m[1mGET /api/chat HTTP/1.1[0m" 405 -
