{'next_action': 'return_group_matching_results', 'matches': [{'index': 1, 'grant_id': 'd328b689-058f-47a8-b290-fd3f7b36bafd', 'grant_title': 'Tactical Behaviors for Autonomous Maneuver', 'agency_name': 'Dept of the Army -- Materiel Command', 'grant_link': 'https://simpler.grants.gov/opportunity/d328b689-058f-47a8-b290-fd3f7b36bafd', 'team': [578, 579], 'team_members': [{'faculty_id': 578, 'faculty_name': 'Alan Fern', 'faculty_email': 'alan.fern@oregonstate.edu'}, {'faculty_id': 579, 'faculty_name': 'Houssam Abbas', 'faculty_email': 'houssam.abbas@oregonstate.edu'}], 'team_score': 19.807301359999997, 'justification': {'one_paragraph': 'The Tactical Behaviors for Autonomous Maneuver Collaborative Research Program (TBAM-CRP) Cycle 2 seeks to develop coordinated behaviors for small teams of autonomous robots to maneuver tactically in complex, contested environments against adversaries. The focus is on "Preparation for Contact"—enabling robot teams to make rapid decisions and execute coordinated maneuvers to achieve tactical advantage when encountering an unknown adversary in non-permissive terrain. Recipients will develop and demonstrate solutions in sophisticated simulations using the ARL Autonomy Stack and Unity-based testbeds, with potential transition to physical platforms at ARL facilities. The program emphasizes collaboration across academia, industry, and government, with $2.1M available annually for multiple awards under a 24-month base period plus optional extensions.', 'why_working_summary': "This team brings complementary expertise in multi-agent autonomous systems, combining Fern's strengths in reinforcement learning, decentralized control, and sim-to-real transfer with Abbas's capabilities in formal verification, safety-critical control, and distributed planning. Together they address the grant's core requirements for developing coordinated tactical behaviors for autonomous agent teams operating in contested environments with adversaries. Fern's experience with multi-robot coordination, terrain-aware locomotion, and learning-based control pairs naturally with Abbas's work on signal temporal logic specifications, probabilistic risk assessment, and fair distributed planning for UAV teams. Their joint capabilities span the full spectrum from learning novel tactics through reinforcement learning to verifying safety constraints and ensuring resilient operation under communication disruptions. This combination positions the team to develop, validate, and demonstrate autonomous maneuver behaviors in sophisticated simulations as required by the TBAM-CRP program.", 'member_roles': [{'faculty_id': 578, 'role': 'Multi-Agent RL and Planning Lead'}, {'faculty_id': 579, 'role': 'Formal Methods and Safety Verification Lead'}], 'coverage': {'strong': [], 'partial': [], 'missing': ['Military tactical doctrine and doctrinal maneuver expertise', 'Game-theoretic adversarial planning and information warfare capabilities', 'ARL autonomy stack and defense simulation environment integration experience']}, 'member_strengths': [{'faculty_id': 578, 'bullets': ['Brings expertise in reinforcement learning for multi-agent coordination and decentralized control, demonstrated through multi-robot cooperative transport without centralized communication. Evidence: Multi-Quadruped Cooperative Object Transport: Learning Decentralized Pinch-Lift-Move (2025) [source=publication].', 'Provides capabilities in vision-based terrain sensing and adaptive locomotion for autonomous agents operating in challenging environments. Evidence: Learning Vision-Based Bipedal Locomotion for Challenging Terrain (2024) [source=publication].', 'Contributes methods for combining deep learning with AI planning for sequential decision-making and policy optimization in complex scenarios. Evidence: Graph Neural Network Based Action Ranking for Planning (2024) [source=publication].', 'Offers experience in sim-to-real transfer and whole-body control for autonomous systems with multi-modal behaviors and balance constraints. Evidence: Sim-to-Real Learning for Humanoid Box Loco-Manipulation (2024) [source=publication].', 'Delivers explainability and acceptance testing frameworks for evaluating learned autonomous system behaviors and decision correctness. Evidence: Beyond Value: CheckList for Testing Inferences in Planning-Based RL (2022) [source=publication].']}, {'faculty_id': 579, 'bullets': ['Provides probabilistic risk assessment and reachability analysis methods for ensuring autonomous vehicle safety under uncertainty. Evidence: PRREACH: Probabilistic Risk Assessment Using Reachability for UAV Control (2025) [source=publication].', 'Contributes signal temporal logic specifications and distributed trajectory planning for multi-UAV coordination with mission objectives. Evidence: Distributed Trajectory Planning for Multi-rotor UAVs with Signal Temporal Logic Objectives (2022) [source=publication].', 'Brings fair distributed planning and deconfliction algorithms for coordinated autonomous aerial systems operating in contested airspace. Evidence: FiReFly: Fair Distributed Receding Horizon Planning for Multiple UAVs (2025) [source=publication].', 'Offers deontic logic frameworks for modeling ethical obligations and constraints in reinforcement learning agents with formal verification. Evidence: Formal Ethical Obligations in Reinforcement Learning Agents: Verification and Policy Updates (2024) [source=publication].', 'Delivers privacy-preserving runtime verification and on-board distributed monitoring for autonomous systems runtime assurance. Evidence: Monitoring in the Dark: Privacy-Preserving Runtime Verification of Cyber-Physical Systems (2025) [source=publication].']}], 'why_not_working': ['The team lacks direct experience with military tactical doctrine and maneuver concepts. While both faculty have strong robotics and multi-agent coordination backgrounds, neither demonstrates familiarity with doctrinal tactics like bounding overwatch, cover and concealment exploitation, or adversarial engagement scenarios that are central to this grant.', "Neither faculty member shows expertise in game-theoretic adversarial planning or covert information control strategies. The grant emphasizes obscuring mission details and controlling information flow during adversarial contact, but the team's work focuses on cooperative coordination, ethical constraints, and safety verification rather than adversarial deception and tactical advantage.", 'The team has limited demonstrated capability in terrain-aware motion planning that exploits military-relevant features. While Fern has vision-based locomotion work, it focuses on traversability rather than tactical positioning using cover, concealment, and viewshed reasoning that the grant requires for autonomous maneuver in contested environments.', "Neither faculty demonstrates experience with the specific ARL autonomy stack, ROS2-based tactical behavior development, or integration with military simulation environments. The grant explicitly requires working with ARL's software suite and simulation tools, but the team's publications show work with general robotics frameworks rather than defense-specific platforms."], 'recommendation': 'This team possesses strong foundational capabilities in multi-agent reinforcement learning, formal verification, and distributed planning that align with core technical requirements. However, critical gaps exist in military tactical doctrine, adversarial game-theoretic planning, and defense-specific platform integration (ARL autonomy stack) that are essential for this program. Before pursuing, the team should partner with collaborators who have military maneuver expertise and experience with defense simulation environments, or consider this opportunity only if willing to invest significantly in acquiring domain-specific tactical knowledge and ARL toolchain familiarity.', 'team_grant_fit': 0.55}}, {'index': 0, 'grant_id': '56e4c5bf-fe1e-4ae1-905b-93a9b53cd8d3', 'grant_title': 'Foundational Research in Robotics', 'agency_name': 'U.S. National Science Foundation', 'grant_link': 'https://simpler.grants.gov/opportunity/56e4c5bf-fe1e-4ae1-905b-93a9b53cd8d3', 'team': [578, 579], 'team_members': [{'faculty_id': 578, 'faculty_name': 'Alan Fern', 'faculty_email': 'alan.fern@oregonstate.edu'}, {'faculty_id': 579, 'faculty_name': 'Houssam Abbas', 'faculty_email': 'houssam.abbas@oregonstate.edu'}], 'team_score': 12.55021202, 'justification': {'one_paragraph': "The Foundational Research in Robotics (FRR) program, jointly led by NSF's CISE and ENG Directorates, funds research on robotic systems that combine computational capability with physical complexity. Proposals must focus on a specific robot or class of robots and demonstrate how the research will endow them with new or significantly enhanced capabilities while addressing fundamental gaps in robotics science. The program emphasizes interdisciplinary research spanning engineering and computer science, with meaningful experimental validation on physical platforms strongly encouraged. Researchers must clearly articulate three key points: the robot focus, the new capabilities to be gained, and the intellectual contribution to foundational robotics knowledge.", 'why_working_summary': 'This team brings complementary expertise in foundational robotics research spanning intelligent control, learning-based autonomy, and formal verification. Alan Fern contributes deep reinforcement learning methods for whole-body humanoid control, multi-robot coordination, and vision-based locomotion over challenging terrain, directly addressing robot intelligence, embodiment, and perception requirements. Houssam Abbas provides formal methods for safety verification, distributed planning with temporal logic specifications, and runtime monitoring for autonomous systems, ensuring robots operate with provable guarantees and ethical constraints. Together, they cover the full spectrum from learning-based capability development to formal assurance, with extensive experimental validation on physical platforms including humanoid robots and UAVs.', 'member_roles': [{'faculty_id': 578, 'role': 'Robot Learning and Control Lead'}, {'faculty_id': 579, 'role': 'Formal Verification and Safety Co-Lead'}], 'coverage': {'strong': [], 'partial': [], 'missing': ['Robot hardware design and mechanical embodiment', 'Robot perception and sensor systems', 'Physical manipulation and human-robot interaction']}, 'member_strengths': [{'faculty_id': 578, 'bullets': ['Develops learned whole-body controllers for humanoid robots executing diverse multi-modal behaviors including locomotion and manipulation. Evidence: Learning Multi-Modal Whole-Body Control for Real-World Humanoid Robots (2024) [source=publication].', 'Creates decentralized cooperative control algorithms enabling multiple robots to transport payloads over rough terrain without centralized communication. Evidence: Multi-Quadruped Cooperative Object Transport: Learning Decentralized Pinch-Lift-Move (2025) [source=publication].', 'Advances vision-based bipedal locomotion using depth sensing and learned heightmap prediction for adaptive terrain traversal. Evidence: Learning Vision-Based Bipedal Locomotion for Challenging Terrain (2024) [source=publication].', 'Implements sim-to-real reinforcement learning for humanoid box manipulation with balance maintenance and whole-body coordination. Evidence: Sim-to-Real Learning for Humanoid Box Loco-Manipulation (2024) [source=publication].', 'Develops explainability and trustworthiness evaluation methods for machine-learned autonomous systems. Evidence: Evaluating Robots Like Human Infants: A Case Study of Learned Bipedal Locomotion (2025) [source=publication].']}, {'faculty_id': 579, 'bullets': ['Provides privacy-preserving runtime verification using formal methods and cryptographic protocols for cyber-physical systems. Evidence: Monitoring in the Dark: Privacy-Preserving Runtime Verification of Cyber-Physical Systems (2025) [source=publication].', 'Develops fair distributed planning and deconfliction algorithms for coordinated autonomous aerial systems with signal temporal logic specifications. Evidence: FiReFly: Fair Distributed Receding Horizon Planning for Multiple UAVs (2025) [source=publication].', 'Creates probabilistic risk assessment and reachability analysis methods for autonomous vehicle safety verification. Evidence: PRREACH: Probabilistic Risk Assessment Using Reachability for UAV Control (2025) [source=publication].', 'Implements formal ethical obligations in reinforcement learning agents with verification and policy update capabilities. Evidence: Formal Ethical Obligations in Reinforcement Learning Agents: Verification and Policy Updates (2024) [source=publication].']}], 'why_not_working': ['The team lacks expertise in physical robot hardware design, mechanical engineering, and embodiment - both faculty focus on algorithms and software with no evidence of designing robot morphologies, actuators, sensors, or physical platforms', 'No demonstrated capability in robot perception systems or sensor integration - missing computer vision for robotics, LIDAR/depth sensing, tactile sensing, and multimodal sensor fusion that are core to robotic systems', "Limited evidence of physical robot experimentation and validation - while Fern has some sim-to-real work on bipedal robots, there's no breadth across robot types, manipulation hardware, or meaningful physical platform development", 'Missing core robotics domains including manipulation and grasping, human-robot interaction and collaboration, and multi-robot coordination hardware - the grant requires robots that physically interact with environments and objects'], 'recommendation': "This team offers strong algorithmic foundations in learning-based control and formal verification, but lacks critical capabilities for foundational robotics research. The absence of robot hardware design expertise, comprehensive perception systems, and breadth in physical experimentation represents fundamental gaps for a program requiring embodied robotic systems. To pursue this opportunity, the team must add partners with expertise in mechanical robot design, multimodal perception and sensor integration, and physical manipulation systems—these are not peripheral but core to the grant's scope.", 'team_grant_fit': 0.45}}, {'index': 2, 'grant_id': '599c5796-9954-4aa0-93d2-4a70f0c432ce', 'grant_title': 'Energy, Power, Control, and Networks', 'agency_name': 'U.S. National Science Foundation', 'grant_link': 'https://simpler.grants.gov/opportunity/599c5796-9954-4aa0-93d2-4a70f0c432ce', 'team': [578, 579], 'team_members': [{'faculty_id': 578, 'faculty_name': 'Alan Fern', 'faculty_email': 'alan.fern@oregonstate.edu'}, {'faculty_id': 579, 'faculty_name': 'Houssam Abbas', 'faculty_email': 'houssam.abbas@oregonstate.edu'}], 'team_score': 14.3202769, 'justification': {'one_paragraph': 'The Energy, Power, Control, and Networks (EPCN) Program from the NSF supports research in modeling, optimization, learning, and control of networked multi-agent systems with applications to energy, transportation, robotics, and biomedical systems. The program emphasizes electric power systems including renewable energy integration, grid resilience, power electronics, and battery management, as well as advanced machine learning and neuromorphic engineering for real-time adaptive systems. Research areas span from fundamental control theory and distributed optimization to practical applications like smart grids, electric vehicles, and microgrids, with particular focus on managing uncertainty, system failures, and the interplay between technical systems and regulatory/economic structures.', 'why_working_summary': "This team combines deep expertise in machine learning, reinforcement learning, and control systems with specialized knowledge in cyber-physical systems verification and distributed autonomous systems. Alan Fern brings extensive experience in adaptive learning algorithms, neural network architectures, and data-driven optimization for complex networked systems, directly addressing the grant's emphasis on dynamic data-enabled learning and control. Houssam Abbas contributes critical capabilities in formal verification, temporal logic specifications, and distributed control for multi-agent coordination under uncertainty, aligning with the program's focus on resilient operation and cyber-physical security. Together, they cover the full spectrum from learning-based control design to formal safety guarantees, spanning applications in energy systems, robotics, and autonomous vehicles that are central to the EPCN program's mission.", 'member_roles': [{'faculty_id': 578, 'role': 'Machine Learning and Control Systems Lead'}, {'faculty_id': 579, 'role': 'Cyber-Physical Systems and Formal Methods Lead'}], 'coverage': {'strong': [], 'partial': [], 'missing': ['Power electronics hardware and electric machine design', 'Electric grid operations and utility-scale energy systems', 'Power system economics and regulatory policy']}, 'member_strengths': [{'faculty_id': 578, 'bullets': ['Develops reinforcement learning and adaptive dynamic programming methods for sequential decision-making and policy optimization in complex systems. Evidence: Reinforcement learning for sequential decision-making and policy optimization [source=publication]', 'Creates attention-based neural network models for spatiotemporal prediction in environmental sensor networks, enabling intelligent monitoring and predictive maintenance. Evidence: Attention mechanisms for spatiotemporal prediction in environmental sensor networks [source=publication]', 'Designs decentralized cooperative control algorithms for multi-robot coordination in manipulation and transport tasks without centralized communication. Evidence: Decentralized cooperative control for multi-robot manipulation and transport tasks [source=publication]', 'Implements constraint-adaptive policy switching techniques for offline safe reinforcement learning deployment under uncertainty and system failures. Evidence: Constraint-adaptive policy switching for offline safe reinforcement learning deployment [source=publication]', 'Develops multi-task learning frameworks combining neural networks with biophysical models for data-limited prediction and optimization problems. Evidence: Multi-task learning for crop phenology and cold hardiness prediction across cultivars [source=publication]']}, {'faculty_id': 579, 'bullets': ['Develops distributed trajectory planning algorithms with signal temporal logic specifications for multi-UAV coordination and mission planning. Evidence: Distributed trajectory planning for multi-UAV missions with signal temporal logic objectives [source=publication]', 'Creates privacy-preserving runtime verification techniques using formal methods and cryptographic protocols for cyber-physical system monitoring. Evidence: Privacy-preserving runtime verification of cyber-physical systems using garbled circuits [source=publication]', 'Designs fair control and deconfliction algorithms for coordinated autonomous aerial systems with distributed planning capabilities. Evidence: Fair distributed planning and deconfliction for coordinated autonomous aerial systems [source=publication]', 'Implements temporal logic falsification and control synthesis methods for cyber-physical systems under uncertainty and disturbances. Evidence: Temporal logic falsification and control synthesis for cyber-physical systems [source=publication]', 'Develops neural network-based inference techniques for learning temporal logic formulas from system behavior traces for intelligent monitoring. Evidence: Neural network inference of temporal logic formulas from system behavior traces [source=publication]']}], 'why_not_working': ['The team lacks hands-on expertise in power electronics hardware design, electric machine design, and grid-tied converter implementation. Both faculty focus on software algorithms (reinforcement learning, temporal logic, control synthesis) rather than physical power system components, leaving critical hardware-focused requirements unaddressed.', "No demonstrated experience with electric power grid operations, renewable energy integration, battery management systems, or microgrid infrastructure. The team's work centers on robotics, UAVs, and agricultural applications, with no evidence of engagement with utility-scale energy systems, grid monitoring, or power system protection.", 'Missing capabilities in power system economics, market design, regulatory policy analysis, and consumer behavior modeling. Neither faculty has published work on electricity markets, demand response, or the socio-economic aspects of smart grids.', 'Limited coverage of neuromorphic engineering and brain-inspired architectures. While both faculty work on machine learning and control, there is no evidence of specialized research in neuromorphic hardware, spiking neural networks, or bio-inspired computing architectures for real-time learning.'], 'recommendation': 'This team should not pursue this grant opportunity. While they bring strong capabilities in machine learning, reinforcement learning, and formal verification for control systems, the EPCN program requires substantial expertise in power electronics hardware, electric grid operations, and utility-scale energy systems that are entirely absent from this team. The focus on robotics and UAV applications does not translate to the power system domain expertise central to this program. To be competitive, the team would need to add multiple faculty with deep backgrounds in power electronics design, grid integration, and energy system operations.', 'team_grant_fit': 0.35}}, {'index': 4, 'grant_id': '7a3287f7-c841-4384-acc9-5194514d701b', 'grant_title': 'Disability and Rehabilitation Engineering', 'agency_name': 'U.S. National Science Foundation', 'grant_link': 'https://simpler.grants.gov/opportunity/7a3287f7-c841-4384-acc9-5194514d701b', 'team': [578, 579], 'team_members': [{'faculty_id': 578, 'faculty_name': 'Alan Fern', 'faculty_email': 'alan.fern@oregonstate.edu'}, {'faculty_id': 579, 'faculty_name': 'Houssam Abbas', 'faculty_email': 'houssam.abbas@oregonstate.edu'}], 'team_score': 12.519817360000001, 'justification': {'one_paragraph': 'The Disability and Rehabilitation Engineering (DARE) program from the NSF supports fundamental engineering research that improves quality of life for persons with disabilities through development of new theories, technologies, and devices. Research must advance knowledge about specific disabilities, pathological motion, or injury mechanisms, with emphasis on neuroengineering, rehabilitation robotics, brain-inspired assistive systems, and novel functional recovery models. The program prioritizes high-risk/high-reward proposals that achieve transformative outcomes rather than incremental improvements, and encourages participatory design with trainees with disabilities. Typical awards are up to three years for single-investigator projects, with proposals accepted year-round; clinical trials are not supported, but feasibility studies with human volunteers may be considered.', 'why_working_summary': "This team combines Alan Fern's expertise in rehabilitation robotics, brain-inspired locomotion control, and assistive system development with Houssam Abbas's capabilities in safety verification, risk assessment, and formal methods for autonomous systems. Fern's work on humanoid robot control, vision-based bipedal locomotion, and sim-to-real transfer directly addresses the grant's emphasis on rehabilitation robotics, neuroengineering, and novel models of functional recovery. Abbas contributes critical expertise in developing safe, verifiable assistive technologies through probabilistic risk assessment, runtime verification, and ethical constraint frameworks. Together, they offer a comprehensive approach to fundamental engineering research that advances transformative disability rehabilitation technologies while ensuring safety and reliability through formal verification methods.", 'member_roles': [{'faculty_id': 578, 'role': 'Rehabilitation Robotics and Locomotion Lead'}, {'faculty_id': 579, 'role': 'Formal Verification and Safety Assurance Consultant'}], 'coverage': {'strong': [], 'partial': [], 'missing': ['Direct experience with disability populations and rehabilitation applications', 'Participatory design and human-centered methods for assistive technology', 'Clinical validation and human subject research in disability contexts']}, 'member_strengths': [{'faculty_id': 578, 'bullets': ['Develops brain-inspired assistive systems through sim-to-real reinforcement learning for humanoid locomotion and whole-body control that model functional recovery mechanisms. Evidence: Sim-to-Real Learning for Humanoid Box Loco-Manipulation (2024) [source=publication].', 'Advances rehabilitation robotics through vision-based bipedal locomotion over challenging terrain using learned heightmap prediction and adaptive control. Evidence: Learning Vision-Based Bipedal Locomotion for Challenging Terrain (2024) [source=publication].', 'Creates novel models of functional recovery through decentralized multi-robot cooperative control for transport and manipulation tasks without centralized communication. Evidence: Multi-Quadruped Cooperative Object Transport: Learning Decentralized Pinch-Lift-Move (2025) [source=publication].', 'Develops computational models for pathological motion through gait optimization and efficiency analysis for high-speed bipedal running with biomechanical comparison. Evidence: Optimizing Bipedal Locomotion for The 100m Dash With Comparison to Human Running (2023) [source=publication].', 'Enables human-centered rehabilitation technology design through natural language-based control for dynamic bipedal robots with interruption robustness and failure mitigation. Evidence: Interruptive Language Control of Bipedal Locomotion (2024) [source=publication].']}, {'faculty_id': 579, 'bullets': ['Provides safety verification capabilities for assistive technologies through probabilistic risk assessment and reachability analysis for autonomous systems. Evidence: PRREACH: Probabilistic Risk Assessment Using Reachability for UAV Control (2025) [source=publication].', 'Develops artificial physiological system models through privacy-preserving runtime verification of cyber-physical systems using formal methods and cryptographic protocols. Evidence: Monitoring in the Dark: Privacy-Preserving Runtime Verification of Cyber-Physical Systems (2025) [source=publication].', 'Enables ethical constraint integration in rehabilitation systems through deontic logic for modeling obligations and policy verification in autonomous agents. Evidence: Formal Ethical Obligations in Reinforcement Learning Agents: Verification and Policy Updates (2024) [source=publication].', 'Advances computational modeling for rehabilitation contexts through temporal logic falsification and control synthesis for cyber-physical systems. Evidence: Falsification and Control of CPS using the Language Set of Discrete-Time Temporal Logic (2025) [source=publication].']}], 'why_not_working': ['The team lacks direct experience with human disability populations and rehabilitation contexts. Their work focuses on general-purpose robotics (bipedal locomotion, humanoid manipulation) and autonomous systems (UAVs, cyber-physical security) without demonstrated application to persons with developmental, cognitive, hearing, mobility, visual, or self-care disabilities.', "No evidence of participatory design methodologies or collaboration with persons with disabilities. The grant strongly encourages including trainees with disabilities and participatory design throughout research, but the team's publications show no engagement with disability communities or human-centered design for assistive technologies.", 'Missing expertise in characterizing specific human functional limitations and pathological motion. While the team has strong computational modeling and robotics capabilities, there is no demonstrated work on understanding injury mechanisms, functional recovery processes, or how disabilities affect human movement and cognition.', "Lack of feasibility studies or human subject research relevant to rehabilitation. The grant supports feasibility studies with human volunteers to validate assistive interventions, but the team's work is primarily simulation-based and hardware-focused without evidence of clinical validation or human trials in disability contexts.", "No demonstrated connection between their technical capabilities and disability-specific engineering interventions. The team's robotics and formal methods expertise could theoretically apply to rehabilitation, but their research portfolio shows no translation of these skills to address quality of life improvements for persons with disabilities."], 'recommendation': "While the team possesses strong technical capabilities in rehabilitation robotics, locomotion control, and safety verification that align with the grant's engineering focus, critical gaps in disability-specific experience make this a poor fit. The absence of direct work with disability populations, participatory design methodologies, clinical validation, and human subject research in rehabilitation contexts fundamentally misaligns with the program's emphasis on transformative interventions for persons with disabilities. Before pursuing this opportunity, the team must establish collaborations with rehabilitation clinicians and disability communities, conduct feasibility studies with human participants, and demonstrate translation of their robotics expertise to address specific functional limitations in target disability populations.", 'team_grant_fit': 0.35}}, {'index': 3, 'grant_id': '40b954c3-f11e-4dda-986a-ac05dae3bb79', 'grant_title': 'Automated Discovery for Design and Control of Turbulent Systems (AutoDIDACTS)', 'agency_name': 'DARPA - Defense Sciences Office', 'grant_link': 'https://simpler.grants.gov/opportunity/40b954c3-f11e-4dda-986a-ac05dae3bb79', 'team': [578, 579], 'team_members': [{'faculty_id': 578, 'faculty_name': 'Alan Fern', 'faculty_email': 'alan.fern@oregonstate.edu'}, {'faculty_id': 579, 'faculty_name': 'Houssam Abbas', 'faculty_email': 'houssam.abbas@oregonstate.edu'}], 'team_score': 51.974146940000004, 'justification': {'one_paragraph': 'AutoDIDACTS seeks to develop data-informed macroscale design principles and surrogate models for aeronautical design and turbulent flow control by combining precision experimental data from compact turbulence simulators with AI-based discovery algorithms. The 48-month program (24-month Phase I base + 24-month Phase II options) aims to overcome limitations of current computational and wind-tunnel approaches by demonstrating generalizable, interpretable models that enable design exploration and optimization across multiple flow regimes (low-Re, transonic, supersonic/hypersonic, and optical communications). Phase I performers must develop high-throughput turbulence simulators, acquire ground-truth experimental data, create automated discovery algorithms, and validate model accuracy and generalizability through independent government verification.', 'why_working_summary': "This team combines deep expertise in machine learning-driven control systems with formal verification and safety-critical system design, directly addressing DARPA's AutoDIDACTS program requirements for automated discovery and optimal control of turbulent aeronautical systems. Alan Fern brings extensive experience in reinforcement learning, physics-informed neural networks, and data-driven model discovery for complex dynamical systems, while Houssam Abbas contributes critical capabilities in formal verification, distributed control synthesis, and probabilistic risk assessment for autonomous aerospace platforms. Together, they offer a comprehensive approach spanning automated discovery algorithms, reduced-order modeling, real-time control implementation, and rigorous validation—essential for developing generalizable principles and revolutionary advances in turbulent flow control beyond incremental improvements.", 'member_roles': [{'faculty_id': 578, 'role': 'Machine Learning & Control Systems Lead'}, {'faculty_id': 579, 'role': 'Verification & Safety Assurance Lead'}], 'coverage': {'strong': [], 'partial': [], 'missing': ['Experimental turbulence and wind tunnel testing capabilities', 'Aeronautical engineering and fluid dynamics domain expertise', 'Computational fluid dynamics and turbulent flow modeling experience']}, 'member_strengths': [{'faculty_id': 578, 'bullets': ['Develops reinforcement learning and attention-based models for spatiotemporal prediction and control in complex dynamical systems, directly applicable to turbulent flow state estimation and real-time adaptive control. Evidence: Attention-Based Models for Snow-Water Equivalent Prediction (2024) [source=publication].', 'Creates physics-informed hybrid neural network frameworks that integrate biophysical models with machine learning for automated parameter calibration and multi-task prediction across diverse operating conditions. Evidence: A Hybrid Modeling Framework for Crop Prediction Tasks via Dynamic Parameter Calibration and Multi-Task Learning (2026) [source=publication].', 'Implements offline safe reinforcement learning with constraint-adaptive policy switching for deployment in safety-critical applications with model uncertainty and operational constraints. Evidence: Constraint-Adaptive Policy Switching for Offline Safe Reinforcement Learning (2025) [source=publication].', 'Designs decentralized multi-robot cooperative control algorithms for transport and manipulation tasks without centralized communication, relevant to distributed control architectures for aeronautical systems. Evidence: Multi-Quadruped Cooperative Object Transport: Learning Decentralized Pinch-Lift-Move (2025) [source=publication].']}, {'faculty_id': 579, 'bullets': ['Develops probabilistic risk assessment and reachability analysis methods for autonomous aerial vehicle control with formal safety guarantees under uncertainty. Evidence: PRREACH: Probabilistic Risk Assessment Using Reachability for UAV Control (2025) [source=publication].', 'Creates distributed trajectory planning and fair deconfliction algorithms for multi-UAV coordination with signal temporal logic mission specifications. Evidence: FiReFly: Fair Distributed Receding Horizon Planning for Multiple UAVs (2025) [source=publication].', 'Implements falsification and control synthesis techniques for cyber-physical systems using temporal logic language sets to ensure specification compliance. Evidence: Falsification and Control of CPS using the Language Set of Discrete-Time Temporal Logic (2025) [source=publication].', 'Designs privacy-preserving runtime verification frameworks for distributed cyber-physical systems enabling on-board monitoring and assurance. Evidence: Monitoring in the Dark: Privacy-Preserving Runtime Verification of Cyber-Physical Systems (2025) [source=publication].']}], 'why_not_working': ['The team lacks hands-on experimental capabilities in turbulent flow systems. The grant requires development and calibration of high-throughput precision turbulence simulators and wind tunnel testing, but neither faculty has demonstrated experience designing or operating experimental fluid dynamics facilities or aeronautical test infrastructure.', "No evidence of aeronautical domain expertise or turbulence physics knowledge. The grant centers on aerodynamic design principles, turbulent boundary layers, and flow control, yet the team's work focuses on robotics, UAV path planning, and formal verification without addressing fundamental fluid mechanics or aeronautical engineering challenges.", "Missing core fluid dynamics modeling and simulation skills. The grant demands computational fluid dynamics integration, physics-informed models for turbulent flows, and reduced-order modeling of aerodynamic systems, but the team's machine learning experience is applied to robotics locomotion and crop prediction rather than fluid systems or aerodynamic optimization.", "Limited alignment with revolutionary aeronautical design goals. The grant explicitly seeks transformative advances beyond evolutionary improvements in aeronautical systems, but the team's research addresses robot control, UAV coordination, and cyber-physical security without tackling aircraft design, flow control actuators, or aerodynamic performance breakthroughs.", 'Weak connection to real-time turbulent flow control deployment. While the team has reinforcement learning and control experience, their applications target bipedal robots and UAV missions rather than the high-speed, real-time control of turbulent boundary layers or aerodynamic surfaces required for operational aircraft systems.'], 'recommendation': "This team should not pursue this opportunity. While they bring strong machine learning and formal verification capabilities, the AutoDIDACTS program fundamentally requires aeronautical engineering expertise, turbulent flow physics knowledge, and experimental fluid dynamics capabilities that are entirely absent from this team. The gap between their robotics/UAV coordination experience and the grant's core focus on revolutionary turbulent flow control for aeronautical design is too substantial to bridge without adding partners with deep fluid dynamics, CFD modeling, and wind tunnel testing expertise.", 'team_grant_fit': 0.25}}, {'index': 5, 'grant_id': 'b39b2236-74f2-466c-875e-8a226a78968c', 'grant_title': 'Mind, Machine and Motor Nexus', 'agency_name': 'U.S. National Science Foundation', 'grant_link': 'https://simpler.grants.gov/opportunity/b39b2236-74f2-466c-875e-8a226a78968c', 'team': [578, 579], 'team_members': [{'faculty_id': 578, 'faculty_name': 'Alan Fern', 'faculty_email': 'alan.fern@oregonstate.edu'}, {'faculty_id': 579, 'faculty_name': 'Houssam Abbas', 'faculty_email': 'houssam.abbas@oregonstate.edu'}], 'team_score': 14.247484700000001, 'justification': {'one_paragraph': 'The Mind, Machine, and Motor Nexus (M3X) program funds fundamental research on bidirectional interactions between humans and intelligent engineered systems operating in physics-based environments. Proposals must advance knowledge in at least one of three areas: conceptual frameworks for understanding human-machine interaction, dynamic interaction analysis and simulation in physical/virtual/hybrid settings, or innovative technologies that enable novel interaction modalities. The program targets complex, dynamic applications such as elder care, disaster response, and dynamic workplaces, emphasizing safety, productivity, and well-being. Research should explore the physical and cognitive principles that enable or constrain human-machine collaboration, advancing foundational theories and technological innovations.', 'why_working_summary': "This team combines deep expertise in intelligent autonomous systems, human-machine interaction, and formal verification methods that directly address the M3X program's core requirements. Alan Fern brings extensive experience in reinforcement learning for humanoid robots, multi-modal sensorimotor integration, and sim-to-real transfer learning that enables bidirectional physical interaction in dynamic environments. Houssam Abbas contributes critical capabilities in formal methods for safety verification, temporal logic specifications, and runtime monitoring that ensure safe operating conditions during human-machine collaboration. Together, they offer complementary strengths spanning theoretical frameworks, experimental paradigms, and technological innovations for physics-based human-machine interaction systems.", 'member_roles': [{'faculty_id': 578, 'role': 'Learning and Interaction Modeling Lead'}, {'faculty_id': 579, 'role': 'Safety and Verification Lead'}], 'coverage': {'strong': [], 'partial': [], 'missing': ['Human factors and cognitive science expertise for analyzing perception, behavior, and mental models during human-machine interaction', 'Experimental methods and testbeds for human-in-the-loop studies evaluating bidirectional interaction with human participants', 'Application experience in human-centered domains such as elder care, disaster response, rehabilitation, or collaborative workplaces']}, 'member_strengths': [{'faculty_id': 578, 'bullets': ['Develops reinforcement learning frameworks for humanoid robots with multi-modal whole-body control enabling diverse bidirectional physical interactions. Evidence: Learning Multi-Modal Whole-Body Control for Real-World Humanoid Robots (2024) [source=publication].', 'Creates vision-based adaptive locomotion systems that integrate real-time sensorimotor data for terrain adaptation in dynamic environments. Evidence: Learning Vision-Based Bipedal Locomotion for Challenging Terrain (2024) [source=publication].', 'Designs decentralized cooperative control algorithms for multi-robot systems that demonstrate learning and co-adaptation in collaborative transport tasks. Evidence: Multi-Quadruped Cooperative Object Transport: Learning Decentralized Pinch-Lift-Move (2025) [source=publication].', 'Establishes explainability and acceptance testing methodologies for evaluating machine-learned autonomous systems with human-centered evaluation frameworks. Evidence: Evaluating Robots Like Human Infants: A Case Study of Learned Bipedal Locomotion (2025) [source=publication].', 'Implements attention-based models for spatiotemporal prediction from sparse sensor networks supporting real-time environmental monitoring. Evidence: Attention-Based Models for Snow-Water Equivalent Prediction (2024) [source=publication].']}, {'faculty_id': 579, 'bullets': ['Develops computational frameworks using deontic logic to model ethical obligations and cognitive constraints in autonomous agents for safe human-machine collaboration. Evidence: Formal Ethical Obligations in Reinforcement Learning Agents: Verification and Policy Updates (2024) [source=publication].', 'Creates privacy-preserving runtime verification systems for distributed cyber-physical systems enabling secure monitoring of human-machine interactions. Evidence: Monitoring in the Dark: Privacy-Preserving Runtime Verification of Cyber-Physical Systems (2025) [source=publication].', 'Designs probabilistic risk assessment and reachability analysis methods for identifying theoretical limits of safe autonomous system operation. Evidence: PRREACH: Probabilistic Risk Assessment Using Reachability for UAV Control (2025) [source=publication].', 'Establishes temporal logic specifications and distributed trajectory planning frameworks for coordinated multi-agent systems with formal safety guarantees. Evidence: FiReFly: Fair Distributed Receding Horizon Planning for Multiple UAVs (2025) [source=publication].', 'Develops neural network-based inference techniques for learning temporal logic formulas from system behavior traces to support interaction analysis. Evidence: Differentiable Inference of Temporal Logic Formulas (2022) [source=publication].']}], 'why_not_working': ['The team lacks expertise in human cognition, perception, and behavior analysis during interaction with intelligent systems. Both faculty focus on autonomous system control and formal verification rather than understanding human cognitive processes, mental models, or behavioral adaptation in human-machine collaboration.', "No demonstrated capability in designing or evaluating interaction modalities (visual, haptic, auditory) for human-machine communication. The team's work centers on robot locomotion and UAV control without addressing how humans perceive, interpret, or respond to system actions through different sensory channels.", 'Missing foundational research in mathematical frameworks specifically for bidirectional human-machine interaction. While both faculty have strong computational modeling skills, their frameworks target autonomous system safety and control rather than modeling the mutual adaptation, co-learning, and reciprocal influence between humans and machines.', 'The team has no experience with human subjects research, user studies, or experimental paradigms involving human participants. All evidence focuses on purely autonomous systems (robots, UAVs) without human-in-the-loop experimentation, which is central to understanding bidirectional interaction phenomena.', "Absence of expertise in creating meaningful task environments that support safe human-machine collaboration in domains like elder care, disaster response, or dynamic workplaces mentioned in the grant. The team's application domains (robot locomotion, UAV coordination, crop prediction) do not involve human collaboration scenarios."], 'recommendation': "This team should not pursue this grant opportunity. While they possess strong technical capabilities in autonomous systems and formal verification, they fundamentally lack the human-centered research expertise that is core to the Mind, Machine and Motor Nexus program. The absence of human factors expertise, human-in-the-loop experimental capabilities, and experience in collaborative human-machine interaction domains represents a critical gap that cannot be easily addressed. The team's strengths in robot control and safety verification alone are insufficient for a program explicitly focused on bidirectional human-machine interaction and understanding human cognitive processes during physical collaboration.", 'team_grant_fit': 0.25}}], 'team_size': 2, 'top_k_grants': None, 'query_text': None, 'broad_category_filter': None, 'agency_filter': None}
